WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.267
-Our first guest tonight
is a Grammy-nominated comedian

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:04.801
who is hosting
"Saturday Night Live" this week,

00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:07.267
right here on NBC,
with musical guest Foo Fighters.

00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:10.834
Please welcome back
to the show Nate Bargatze.

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:13.334
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:29.334
Welcome back, man.
-What's up, man?

00:00:29.334 --> 00:00:31.167
-I'm so --
-Thank you.

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:33.834
-Hey, bear!
-Hey, bear. Hello, folks.

00:00:33.834 --> 00:00:35.067
-I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:37.334
I'm so happy the reason
you're in New York --

00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:38.400
you're hosting "SNL."

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.000
-Yes.
-This is such a blast.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.067
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:44.167
It's -- Yeah, it's insane, dude.

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:47.133
It was like -- It's almost --
When they asked, you're like,

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:49.801
"I don't know if I'm famous
enough to do that."

00:00:49.801 --> 00:00:52.067
Like, you want to -- Almost,
I want to tell them that.

00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:53.133
They're like,
"We're going to get you."

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.934
I was like,
"Guys, I appreciate it,

00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:57.801
but there's some other people
I think y'all should go after."

00:00:57.801 --> 00:00:59.100
You're like,
"Your show has a legacy.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.234
You don't want to mess
around with that."

00:01:00.234 --> 00:01:01.667
-You're "SNL, dude."

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:05.000
I go, "Look, thanks for
the offer, but, you know...

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.100
I'm doing you a favor."

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.300
-You've played -- Obviously,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.567
you've played
New York City a ton.

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:11.334
You've played big rooms
in New York City a ton.

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:14.133
How are the nerves
on Tuesday here?

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:15.801
-So far, it's good.

00:01:15.801 --> 00:01:17.400
This was the first day --
I mean, you know what?

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.501
They called me Sunday
about, like, a sketch idea.

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:24.467
And I was like, "Oh, man,
it's, like, happening."

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:25.901
Like, it was Sunday.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:28.868
-And then, Monday, you come in,
and, I mean, you really --

00:01:28.868 --> 00:01:30.501
I'm really waiting
for them to be like,

00:01:30.501 --> 00:01:32.100
"We're not going to air this."

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.968
Like, they're -- Like,
I'm just some trial show

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:36.234
that they're like,

00:01:36.234 --> 00:01:39.100
"We just wanted to make sure
the cameras worked."

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.234
Someone -- Like,
Vin Diesel's backstage.

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:42.534
And I'm like, "Is he on?"

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:43.701
And you're like,
"What's he doing?"

00:01:43.701 --> 00:01:45.200
You're like,
"Don't worry about him."

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.133
-He just doesn't like
doing dress anymore.

00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:48.300
And you're like,
"These are a lot of

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.367
Vin Diesel-type sketches."

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:52.767
-I don't know if I
can sell these, dude.

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:56.133
-I will say, when I was there
on the writing staff,

00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:59.133
the greatest feeling was
when a stand-up would show up

00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:01.901
because you knew they would
handle their own monologue.

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:03.400
Do you --
Is that safe to assume

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.801
that you were going to
write your own monologue?

00:02:04.801 --> 00:02:07.968
-Yeah, yeah. That's the only
thing I can do that's good.

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:11.601
Like, I ran it last night.

00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:12.634
It was great
because I got to, like --

00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:13.801
You know,
I've been on the road,

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:15.000
so it's like you're doing
an hour, and then --

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.901
So, last night, I came in,
and last night

00:02:17.901 --> 00:02:19.968
I ran around and did spots

00:02:19.968 --> 00:02:21.267
and, you know, trying to
figure out --

00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:22.734
The one idea I thought I was
going to do --

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:24.133
it's kind of changed.

00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:25.367
And so then you're like --
You're just kind of trying to

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:27.734
figure out the --
you know, rough eight minutes

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:29.367
or so that we're going to work.

00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:30.934
-When you
run around New York City,

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:32.534
so you're going to different
clubs, you're doing spots.

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:34.467
Do you let them know, "Hey,

00:02:34.467 --> 00:02:36.934
I'm going to be doing this
on Saturday at 'SNL.'"

00:02:36.934 --> 00:02:38.767
Do you give them that preface?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:43.267
-The host said -- I mean, like,
if I go up and go,

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:45.033
"This will be on 'SNL.'"

00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:46.534
And then they go...

00:02:46.534 --> 00:02:48.133
I would, just go,
"Just so you know,

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:52.701
this is on 'Saturday Night Live'
this Saturday."

00:02:52.701 --> 00:02:55.868
It's -- You do --
I mean, I remember

00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:57.334
following a lot of comics
that would come in

00:02:57.334 --> 00:03:00.701
and run in for a spot,
and so it's very cool.

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:02.767
You do want the audience
to know -- I would do it

00:03:02.767 --> 00:03:04.667
with late-night sets,
when you'd run those to be like,

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:06.033
"Just tell them
this is what I'm doing."

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:07.868
So the audience understands
you're doing 5 minutes

00:03:07.868 --> 00:03:08.868
or 8 minutes, whatever it is.

00:03:08.868 --> 00:03:09.934
It's very tight.

00:03:09.934 --> 00:03:11.801
It's just a little
different feeling.

00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:13.567
But you have to get back
into the New York rhythm.

00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:15.868
Like, the New York rhythm --
Because when I lived here,

00:03:15.868 --> 00:03:17.400
it's very --
You know, it's, like, quick.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.334
It's like you've got to be
just on top of them.

00:03:19.334 --> 00:03:21.734
And, you know, instead of, like,
when you do a long show,

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.067
it's, like, a little more.

00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:26.834
And so, I mean, I had to,
like -- I did five sets,

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:31.734
and it took me a good three
just to get back into New York.

00:03:31.734 --> 00:03:34.000
One of them did not go good.
-Yeah.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.868
-It's so funny because people
will say to me sometimes,

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:38.667
"Oh, my God,
I can't even imagine

00:03:38.667 --> 00:03:40.434
what it's like to go onstage
and do an hour."

00:03:40.434 --> 00:03:43.567
And you're like, "Weirdly,
it's easier than doing five."

00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:44.634
-Yes.

00:03:44.634 --> 00:03:46.334
-Because an hour,
they get to know you.

00:03:46.334 --> 00:03:47.868
You know,
realize they're not seeing

00:03:47.868 --> 00:03:49.701
the best five minutes first.

00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:51.667
But when you've got to do five,
they're like, "Wait.

00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:53.133
This is the five?"

00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:54.901
-Yeah, this is it.

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.834
They go, "You're doing
that this Saturday?"

00:03:56.834 --> 00:04:00.267
I get a couple of that.

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:03.334
They're going, "You're doing
this on -- Alright."

00:04:03.334 --> 00:04:04.767
-And they're like,
"Honey, I think we can

00:04:04.767 --> 00:04:06.133
wake up early on Sunday,

00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:09.200
because we're going to get
a good night's sleep."

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.868
-People will just tune in
for the sketches.

00:04:10.868 --> 00:04:11.968
They're like,
"We'll watch the sketch."

00:04:11.968 --> 00:04:14.100
It's like,
that's the worst I'm at.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.501
They're like, "But we've seen
this other, so..."

00:04:17.501 --> 00:04:20.300
-Now, a lot of people might
think you're flying high

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:23.000
because you're hosting "SNL,"
but you had an allergic reaction

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.300
in Wichita.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.334
Was this before a show?

00:04:25.334 --> 00:04:27.133
-This was an hour
before the show.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:28.467
-Okay.

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:30.300
-I tried to go running,
tried to do the right thing

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.033
for my dumb body.

00:04:32.033 --> 00:04:33.901
That's what's infuriating,
because you go,

00:04:33.901 --> 00:04:36.133
"I'm doing what
I'm supposed to do, man,"

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:38.400
and then your body falls --
Like, I don't --

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.801
I don't remember ever
being allergic to anything.

00:04:40.801 --> 00:04:43.968
And I guess you just get
old and weak

00:04:43.968 --> 00:04:47.267
and you can't handle air and...

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:49.000
-So, this is --
You posted a photo of yourself.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.400
You look pretty, uh, yeah.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.734
Was it mid-run that you realized
it was happening or...

00:04:53.734 --> 00:04:55.501
-We did --
We were having to do two laps.

00:04:55.501 --> 00:04:58.667
So, it was mid-run, we go back.
-Those are those mile laps?

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:01.534
-Yeah. Yeah, mile.

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:05.567
We do two laps around the bus.
And...

00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:08.467
-It's a big bus.
-It's a big bus, everybody.

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:10.300
So, I go back.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.501
My hands start itching.
My feet are itching.

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:15.467
And it's Wichita, which has
some of the most allergies.

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:17.734
Like, you know, and those people
are some strong people

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:19.934
because they're breathing in
that stuff.

00:05:19.934 --> 00:05:22.133
And it was, like, ragweed.
I guess it just hit me wrong.

00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:24.467
So that goes up -- I mean,
we're an hour before the show,

00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:25.567
and it's like this.

00:05:25.567 --> 00:05:27.033
I was like, "This is not good."

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:29.234
And, so, then my buddy is like,
"You should take Benadryl."

00:05:29.234 --> 00:05:30.934
But I was like, "That's going
to make me fall asleep.

00:05:30.934 --> 00:05:32.133
You know,
I don't want to take it."

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:33.767
We got an EMT came down,

00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:35.667
and he was a super-nice guy,
bless his heart.

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:37.434
But it was his first day.
-Yeah.

00:05:37.434 --> 00:05:39.701
-And so he was not --

00:05:39.701 --> 00:05:41.567
He doesn't have
the stuff for this.

00:05:41.567 --> 00:05:43.501
-Yeah.
-And, so, he just came in --

00:05:43.501 --> 00:05:45.100
-By the way,
that's a different person.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.400
-It's a different person.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:48.033
And, so, he came in
and was like, "I don't know."

00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:49.167
You know...

00:05:49.167 --> 00:05:51.534
And then he didn't know --

00:05:51.534 --> 00:05:53.701
Like, you know,
if you work at --

00:05:53.701 --> 00:05:54.801
you don't know who everybody is.

00:05:54.801 --> 00:05:56.000
-Sure.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.100
-And, so, he comes down there
and then he goes,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.834
"So, your name is
Travis Mathew,"

00:05:59.834 --> 00:06:02.000
because I have this shirt on.

00:06:03.767 --> 00:06:06.133
It's just the brand.

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:07.901
And I just said, "Yeah."

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:09.901
I just went with it
because it's --

00:06:09.901 --> 00:06:11.601
You know, am I going to go
into a whole thing about it?

00:06:11.601 --> 00:06:14.033
You know, I was like, "Just
write, yeah, Travis Mathew."

00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:15.200
You know?

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.300
Like, "I'm Ralph Lauren. I am.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.067
My friends call me Polo."

00:06:20.067 --> 00:06:24.567
So, I'll just do
whatever that guy said.

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:27.267
-That's weirdly -- The most
insulting thing is he thought

00:06:27.267 --> 00:06:29.434
you were a guy who would just
have your own name.

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:32.801
-Yeah. That into my shirt to --

00:06:32.801 --> 00:06:35.567
We ended up getting --
Another doctor came, Dr. Sean.

00:06:35.567 --> 00:06:39.100
Goes-by-first-name-only
doctor, so that's --

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.767
-Those are the guys you get
at venues right before a show.

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:42.033
-Yeah.

00:06:42.033 --> 00:06:43.133
-They're, like,
first-name doctors.

00:06:43.133 --> 00:06:44.200
-Those, you got to be like,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.467
"Where's the bottle
for these pills?"

00:06:46.467 --> 00:06:49.300
And there's just no bottle,
because it's all --

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.901
He goes, "I got a few things
down here."

00:06:52.901 --> 00:06:55.234
-Yeah, Dr. Sean doesn't
like follow-up questions.

00:06:55.234 --> 00:06:58.667
-Yeah, he doesn't.
He goes, "Dr. Sean."

00:06:58.667 --> 00:07:03.567
I go, "Can I see your ID?
He goes, "I left it in the car."

00:07:03.567 --> 00:07:05.234
But he was very nice, too.

00:07:05.234 --> 00:07:06.234
And, like, he gave --
And I ended up

00:07:06.234 --> 00:07:07.367
having to take Benadryl.

00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:08.534
Then I fall asleep.

00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:11.100
And, so, then I had to
give my assistant --

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:12.300
I was like, "You got
to wake me up, dude,

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.667
like, if I fall asleep."

00:07:13.667 --> 00:07:15.934
And I fell asleep hard
because of Benadryl.

00:07:15.934 --> 00:07:17.400
And then I did -- And I got up.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.501
I mean, I was --
Within eight minutes

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:22.300
of being the most asleep,

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:25.367
I was just in front
of 6,000 people.

00:07:25.367 --> 00:07:28.634
-And were you cognizant
of that whole act of like,

00:07:28.634 --> 00:07:30.133
"I'm out of it," or did you --

00:07:30.133 --> 00:07:31.334
once you
got in front of the audience,

00:07:31.334 --> 00:07:33.133
did it kind of lock in,
like muscle memory?

00:07:33.133 --> 00:07:35.000
-Yeah, it went in.
You know what?

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.267
The show was great.

00:07:36.267 --> 00:07:38.834
I almost think it was
a lot more freeing.

00:07:38.834 --> 00:07:40.634
Like, I was all --
You know, I'm on Benadryl.

00:07:40.634 --> 00:07:42.200
I'm like -- I'm up there like,

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.300
"We're having a pretty good time
tonight, folks."

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:47.968
Like, I go, "Nice and easy,"
you know?

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:49.400
My wife told me --

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:51.834
Because I was telling her this,
and she said --

00:07:51.834 --> 00:07:53.033
Because we're worried
about being nervous

00:07:53.033 --> 00:07:54.167
on "Saturday Night Live."

00:07:54.167 --> 00:07:55.300
And she was like,
"Well, if you can do this,

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:56.634
you might be all right."

00:07:56.634 --> 00:08:00.434
Like, just to wake up and
then go talk to 6,000 people.

00:08:00.434 --> 00:08:01.767
But they were awesome.

00:08:01.767 --> 00:08:04.400
-I will tell you, after --
If between dress and air,

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.467
you'll know dress
didn't go well if Lorne goes,

00:08:06.467 --> 00:08:07.868
"Maybe some Benadryl?

00:08:07.868 --> 00:08:09.734
Do you want it?"
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:09.734 --> 00:08:10.934
A whole bottle.

00:08:10.934 --> 00:08:12.701
-He goes,
"I've heard Benadryl helps."

00:08:12.701 --> 00:08:14.968
-Dr. Sean's there.
I go, "How'd you find him?"

00:08:14.968 --> 00:08:16.167
-Oh, yeah.

00:08:16.167 --> 00:08:18.701
Oh, trust me, Lorne's got
a bunch of Dr. Seans.

00:08:22.901 --> 00:08:24.067
You mentioned your wife.

00:08:24.067 --> 00:08:27.734
I've always talked about
I'm so such a fan

00:08:27.734 --> 00:08:31.400
of the material you do
about her onstage.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.968
She does not seem to be someone
who puts you on a pedestal,

00:08:33.968 --> 00:08:35.367
based on the material
you do about it.

00:08:35.367 --> 00:08:38.868
Is she excited, though,
about Saturday?

00:08:38.868 --> 00:08:41.033
-Very excited.
This one's very exciting.

00:08:41.033 --> 00:08:43.100
I mean, we've been together
before I ever started comedy,

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:47.200
so it's -- You know, I think
that's a big reason for that.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.167
And -- But she's very excited
about "Saturday Night Live."

00:08:50.167 --> 00:08:54.200
This is definitely something.
It really is crazy.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.467
It's -- I mean,
I can't even des--

00:08:56.467 --> 00:08:59.801
Like, you're just really shocked
that I'm even here.

00:08:59.801 --> 00:09:03.000
And so everybody has been --
Everybody is super-excited.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.400
She's a big fan
of the Foo Fighters, as I am --

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:06.868
-Yes. That's an exciting thing,
as well.

00:09:06.868 --> 00:09:09.667
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:09:09.667 --> 00:09:12.200
That was probably her most
excitement, was that.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:13.701
Like, I feel like
that's what impressed her.

00:09:13.701 --> 00:09:15.300
-Yeah.
-I didn't even choose.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.567
It's like I went in and go,
"I'd like the Foo Fighters."

00:09:18.567 --> 00:09:21.801
This is my first time hosting.
And the Foo Fighters, please.

00:09:21.801 --> 00:09:23.133
-She heard that.
She was like,

00:09:23.133 --> 00:09:24.701
"Oh, that's how they can get
away with you hosting."

00:09:24.701 --> 00:09:26.167
-Yeah, exactly.
-"They got the Foo Fighters."

00:09:26.167 --> 00:09:28.901
-Oh, yeah. It's going to be
all Dave Grohl in every sketch.

00:09:28.901 --> 00:09:32.868
I'm just kind of on the show.

00:09:32.868 --> 00:09:35.968
Yeah, so, very excited.
I get a lot of sketch ideas.

00:09:35.968 --> 00:09:38.033
Do you get --
-Oh, from, like, family and --

00:09:38.033 --> 00:09:40.467
-Oh, just every -- I mean,
this is everybody's dream

00:09:40.467 --> 00:09:42.734
is to somehow have an in.

00:09:42.734 --> 00:09:44.300
Because, I mean,
I know it because I've --

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:45.701
You've watched "Saturday Night
Live" your whole life,

00:09:45.701 --> 00:09:47.734
and you're like, "I've got
a pretty good sketch idea."

00:09:47.734 --> 00:09:49.567
And so then once
you get a friend

00:09:49.567 --> 00:09:51.100
that's like, "You know,
I'm going to be there,"

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:54.100
they're like,
"Well, let me let it fly."

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:57.501
And it's just been text
and e-mail of just like,

00:09:57.501 --> 00:09:58.701
"Run this by everybody."

00:09:58.701 --> 00:10:02.300
Like, it just --
Nobody's a professional writer.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:05.667
-Anything helpful or just does
it all feel like spam mail?

00:10:05.667 --> 00:10:07.734
-It's all --
Like, I'm going through it.

00:10:07.734 --> 00:10:09.701
I mean, some are like,
"Yeah, that's funny."

00:10:09.701 --> 00:10:11.667
Then some are like, "Dude,
who do you think we are?

00:10:11.667 --> 00:10:13.367
Like, you think I can just
walk in to

00:10:13.367 --> 00:10:15.033
'Saturday Night Live,'
professional writers,

00:10:15.033 --> 00:10:19.434
and just be like,
'Hey, my buddy Michael --

00:10:19.434 --> 00:10:22.767
he took some improv
classes 40 years ago

00:10:22.767 --> 00:10:24.834
and he's got some good ideas.

00:10:24.834 --> 00:10:27.133
I'd like to --
I got him on the horn

00:10:27.133 --> 00:10:29.934
if y'all want to talk to him.'"

00:10:29.934 --> 00:10:32.467
-Well, I hope that they do talk
to Michael just for that, yeah.

00:10:32.467 --> 00:10:34.667
-I, do too. Yeah.
-Hey, I'm so excited.

00:10:34.667 --> 00:10:36.434
I think everybody's
going to be so excited.

00:10:36.434 --> 00:10:38.701
Thanks so much for making time
for us on the busy week.

00:10:38.701 --> 00:10:40.033
-You're the best, man.

00:10:40.033 --> 00:10:43.300
-You guys, Nate Bargatze,
"SNL" Saturday, October 28th,

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:45.501
with the Foo Fighters,
on NBC and live on Peacock.

00:10:45.501 --> 00:10:46.667
Stick around.

00:10:46.667 --> 00:10:47.968
We'll be right back
with Andrew Rannells.

